        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Caporetto</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">24 October to 19 November 1917</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">Near the town of Kobarid (Caporetto, Karfreit) and the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soca">Isonzo</a>, then in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-littoral">Austrian Littoral</a>, now in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>; the Italians then fell back about 150 kilometres to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piave">Piave</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a>, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luigi-cadorna">Luigi Cadorna</a>, with the Second Army commanded by Luigi Capello, against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a>: Austro-Hungarian Army Group Boroević under Svetozar Boroević and a new 14th Army under the German Otto von Below, made up of nine Austrian and six to eight German divisions. The Italian army's effective strength was 1,800,000 before the battle.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Italian Second Army collapsed and the Italian army retreated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piave">Piave</a>, its effective strength falling to 1,000,000; Cadorna was forced to resign and the government of Paolo Boselli fell.</span></div>
        </div>
