    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The attack opened with gas fired from 894 tubes dug into a reverse slope, followed by a bombardment from 2,200 guns and an advance by specially trained stormtrooper units, which broke the Italian line between the IV and XXVII Corps.</li><li>Italian losses were 13,000 killed, 30,000 wounded and 265,000 to 275,000 prisoners, against about 70,000 casualties for the Austro-Hungarians and Germans.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a>' supply lines were stretched to breaking point and the advance stopped at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piave">Piave</a>, where the Italians held, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> went on to defeat the Austro-Hungarian army at Vittorio <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veneto">Veneto</a> in 1918.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In Italian the name Caporetto came to stand for any terrible defeat: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> called the failed general strike of 1922 the &quot;Caporetto of Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialism">Socialism</a>&quot;.</p></aside>
    </section>

